WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:03.845
Now we move from fiber to copper.

2
00:00:04.595 --> 00:00:10.483
Every TPU rack sends six copper cables to its
end of row rack.

3
00:00:11.833 --> 00:00:18.790
We will cover what copper does here, the six
runs, both ends of each run, and how to tell

4
00:00:18.790 --> 00:00:21.839
runs apart when they all look the same.

5
00:00:25.989 --> 00:00:32.730
The fiber you have learned so far carries the
AI data: huge amounts, very fast.

6
00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:35.805
The copper does a different job.

7
00:00:37.205 --> 00:00:40.427
It connects the rack to the management
network.

8
00:00:41.177 --> 00:00:48.701
That is a separate network people use to set
up, watch and control the equipment, even when

9
00:00:48.701 --> 00:00:51.182
the main data network is down.

10
00:00:53.482 --> 00:00:56.405
Some of the runs are console connections.

11
00:00:57.155 --> 00:01:04.692
A console port gives a direct, text-only
connection to a device, used for first setup

12
00:01:04.692 --> 00:01:06.200
and for recovery.

13
00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:15.384
The copper runs land on panels in the end of
row rack, and patch cords connect them to

14
00:01:15.384 --> 00:01:17.225
pre-installed switches.

15
00:01:18.575 --> 00:01:24.549
From there, fiber uplinks carry the management
traffic to the management spines.

16
00:01:25.299 --> 00:01:27.261
That is lesson thirteen.

17
00:01:31.411 --> 00:01:33.353
Here are all six runs.

18
00:01:34.103 --> 00:01:36.492
Take a moment to read the table.

19
00:01:38.992 --> 00:01:46.298
From the top of rack switch: its ETH
management port goes to the copper panel at RU

20
00:01:46.298 --> 00:01:50.875
twenty-four, which feeds the management leaf
switch.

21
00:01:52.225 --> 00:01:58.305
Its console port goes to the panel at RU
thirty, for the console switch.

22
00:01:59.655 --> 00:02:07.888
From each TROSS unit: the GIG E 1 port goes to
the panel at RU twenty-two, for the TROSS leaf

23
00:02:07.888 --> 00:02:08.508
switch.

24
00:02:09.858 --> 00:02:15.191
And each TROSS unit's console port goes to RU
thirty as well.

25
00:02:16.541 --> 00:02:20.211
So all four console runs go to RU thirty.

26
00:02:20.961 --> 00:02:27.130
The guide checked these on all two thousand
five hundred ninety-two copper runs in the

27
00:02:27.130 --> 00:02:27.489
hall.

28
00:02:31.639 --> 00:02:34.519
Each copper cable is a home run.

29
00:02:35.269 --> 00:02:43.963
It has an RJ45 plug at the TPU rack, and runs
straight to a jack on the end of row copper

30
00:02:43.963 --> 00:02:44.549
panel.

31
00:02:45.299 --> 00:02:48.307
There is no panel at the TPU end.

32
00:02:50.607 --> 00:02:54.937
On each TROSS unit, look for the port labeled
console.

33
00:02:56.287 --> 00:02:59.338
Then find the port labeled GIG E 1.

34
00:03:00.088 --> 00:03:06.851
The guide notes that port positions in its
drawing are simplified, so go by the label.

35
00:03:08.201 --> 00:03:13.875
TROSS unit one is on side A, and TROSS unit
two is on side B.

36
00:03:14.625 --> 00:03:16.993
Both are at RU thirty-eight.

37
00:03:21.143 --> 00:03:28.141
At the end of row rack, each home run ends on
a jack in the copper panel next to its switch.

38
00:03:29.491 --> 00:03:34.184
You terminate each home run on the jack the
cutsheet gives you.

39
00:03:34.934 --> 00:03:39.414
To terminate means to attach the cable's wires
to the jack.

40
00:03:41.714 --> 00:03:46.514
Then a short CAT6 patch cord connects the jack
to the switch.

41
00:03:47.264 --> 00:03:50.421
The jack number is the switch port number.

42
00:03:51.171 --> 00:03:53.497
Jack ten goes to port ten.

43
00:03:54.847 --> 00:04:01.481
In the vertical cable manager, cables exit
between the fingers closest to the switch.

44
00:04:02.231 --> 00:04:13.303
Bundle with half-inch Velcro every twelve to
eighteen inches in the tray, per DRS 01.00.03.

45
00:04:14.053 --> 00:04:16.208
Nothing touches the floor.

46
00:04:17.558 --> 00:04:24.472
Label every copper cable with a flag label,
four inches from the plug, at each end,

47
00:04:24.472 --> 00:04:26.305
printed on both sides.

48
00:04:27.655 --> 00:04:33.970
The guide does not say which termination
standard to use, or whether copper must be

49
00:04:33.970 --> 00:04:34.503
tested.

50
00:04:35.253 --> 00:04:37.983
Ask your lead what your site requires.

51
00:04:42.133 --> 00:04:47.211
All six runs are the same kind of cable, often
the same color.

52
00:04:47.961 --> 00:04:52.569
What makes each one different is where it
starts and where it ends.

53
00:04:53.969 --> 00:05:01.306
The switch's ETH and console ports are side by
side, but they go to different panels:

54
00:05:01.306 --> 00:05:03.291
twenty-four and thirty.

55
00:05:04.641 --> 00:05:11.319
On a TROSS unit, GIG E 1 goes to twenty-two,
and console goes to thirty.

56
00:05:12.669 --> 00:05:19.346
TROSS one and TROSS two are on opposite sides
of the rack, and their labels say which.

57
00:05:20.696 --> 00:05:24.173
And do not confuse a home run with a patch
cord.

58
00:05:24.923 --> 00:05:28.187
The home run comes from the TPU rack.

59
00:05:28.937 --> 00:05:33.247
The patch cord is the short cable from jack to
switch.

60
00:05:34.597 --> 00:05:42.511
The label is how you confirm each one: line
one, the A device and port, and line two, the

61
00:05:42.511 --> 00:05:44.111
Z device and port.

62
00:05:48.261 --> 00:05:54.939
In lesson twelve of the course, match each run
to its panel and spot copper mistakes.

